we sometimes joke around about this  phrase the Battle of ideas and I don't  know where it actually became  popularized I know that Dave Rubin on  the Rubin report uses it and it's become  sort of satirized in some ways and I  know that there are also other online  movements that focus on free speech that  talk about the battle of ideas but I  don't want to use it satirically here  because I actually want to talk about  something really serious that's going on  what I'm referring to when I talk about  the movement around the battle of ideas  and principles is sort of this  philosophy this overarching kind of  de-facto philosophy that has developed  where it's really popular to say what  matters most and arguably sometimes they  say what matters exclusively are the  underlying principles that we stand for  and that policy is sort of secondary  from that it flows naturally from our  principles and our ideas but you have to  first before you can really talk about  policy have your principles and ideas  straight and that's the tough work and  the rest is sort of easy and it often is  applied by the extreme forms of  libertarianism it also applies sometimes  like one example would be there's this  guy online called Carl Benjamin who  started a movement around sort of naming  and compiling the principles that  so-called classic liberals or true  liberals as he refers to them or  something like that what they stand for  and he's also bring critical actually of  some progressives for supposedly not  even knowing what they stand for that  they're only focused on policy and they  don't know what their principles are and  this movement that has an obsession with  the principles and ideas is really  really misguided and to be clear I'm not  saying principles are irrelevant and  that ideas shouldn't be discussed and  that it shouldn't be clear to one sort  of what their guiding principles are I  think it's very important to understand  what principles underlie our political  philosophy our life philosophy its  absence  lutely a sort of anchor or a set of  pillars upon which one can base more  specific policy positions why would  someone be for or against abortion well  you've got to understand sort of what  principles and basic ideas are at play  in your personal philosophy to then  guide you in making that decision but  this movement has expanded into  something that has actually become a  replacement for making difficult  decisions about policy when the  principles conflict which is almost all  the time almost all the time no set of  principles is going to give you the  answers to what it is that should happen  in society in the world of politics this  ideas movement overlaps in some ways  even though most of them would don't  know it they have no idea that it does  it's sort of overlaps with the positive  but with positivism the movement of  positivism sort of overlaps with the  principles of universalism which assert  that there are ideas with universal  application without really needing to  look at all the details of a particular  situation you've got your universal  principles and they apply everywhere and  give you the answers and I believe that  this ideas and principles movement is a  cop out it's a cop out from having to  really figure out difficult realities  that exist in the world and I want to be  clear this is not some postmodernist  cultural relativist whew right I'm not  praising or criticizing post-modernism  or cultural relativism in this segment  that's just not what this is plain and  simple and I'll try to make it clearer  with an example our friend Greg Stevens  emailed me a good example a couple  months ago of this lots of these  classical liberal pseudo libertarian  types will often say that two of their  guiding principles are the right to life  and the right to property and property  usually includes both physical property  and money the money that you earned all  right sounds good right nice principles  so to have a right to life in society  you really need a police force to  enforce that  there's going to be a right to life  through punishment for people who  violate the right to life of others as  an example if you're a police officer  you need to get paid because you need to  eat you need to live you need to feed  your family so very quickly you see that  to enforce the right to life of  everybody you need police and they need  a salary well okay if you say we'll put  a mandatory tax on people to pay for the  police  well according to many libertarians  you're violating the right to property  because you are taking the earned money  of people without their consent to pay  for police if you want to take a  different approach you say well this is  a libertarian utopia so you don't have  to fund the police people can choose to  fund the police but you only get police  protection if you pay well poor people  might want to fund the police but they  can't afford to so now if you're poor  you don't get the same right to life  that everybody else gets because you  can't afford it in modern Western  societies we've decided okay well  taxation to pay for the police is not  optional right we've decided in Western  democracies that it's more important to  have police to defend our right to life  and the police to protect everyone and  some of the freedom to decide what is  done with 100% of your money has been  removed you are going to if you're  earning wages pay for the police through  taxation how would the set of principles  give us that answer right remember going  back to our two principles they were a  right to life and a right to property  but as a society the decision was made  we're going to take away a little bit of  your right to property to prop up the  right to life  there's no set of principles you can  write down that will take into account  the complicated realities of the real  world period thinking that your  principles will give you the answer to  every political question is ridiculous  and where it gets dangerous is that  entire movements are being built around  the idea of unwavering and fixed black  and white principles and people aren't  doing the thinking anymore about the  CLEC cities of the real world a lot of  the so-called red pilling online  movement presents itself a sort of  exposing you to knowledge instead of the  sheltered ignorant that you've been  living under for your whole life but  it's often selective narratives that  will very frequently foment your belief  in some quite frankly arbitrary set of  principles without actually equipping  equipping you with the reality that  you're gonna need to deal with  complicated and messy issues in the  world it's actually a form of pseudo  intellectual ism in that by telling  people that they're doing the hard and  sophisticated work of establishing their  principles that they're really thinking  hard but the principles are actually the  easy part it's very easy to say our  principles are freedom or equality or  security or education or whatever right  but the real thinking is applying the  principles especially when they conflict  which is almost all the time  in the real world and I'd argue that  that's the more difficult part that we  should be focused on airport security  for example right we have a society  where our principles my philosophy is I  value security and I value freedom so  what do you do about airport security  which do you value more in what  proportions does the lack of freedom  that we have as a result of TSA and  no-fly lists and everything else that  there is does that loss of freedom  equate to or a does it seem valuable to  you in terms of the security that it  might add if we believe that it actually  gives us security just the principles  aren't going to get us anywhere  and people need to think way harder  about real world situations than the  principles of security and freedom when  we're talking about abortion except in  cases of absolute and total religious  dogma that is completely anti-abortion  which is that what I would which I would  argue actually excuses you from really  having to think hard most people  who have views on abortion that are  somewhere on the spectrum actually need  to evaluate multiple principles that  guide them in their lives these are not  simple answers and the idea that you're  gonna watch some YouTube videos and  develop some philosophy with a list of  five ten or fifteen principles and then  you don't really need to think hard  about every issue that's out there  because you're gonna get all the answers  from those principles is absolutely and  totally misguided and I think ultimately  we are dumbing down the political debate  through this movement which claims it  claims to empower people to really think  hard it's a problem I hope you'll send  me your feedback and thoughts about what  you think I'm very worried about that  movement I'm on Twitter at D Pakman the  show is on twitter at david pakman show  we will take a break and be back after  this with plenty more the video you just  finished watching was made possible by  you through the membership program you  can sign up for membership at david  pakman.com slash membership we are  viewer supported independent media you  can use the coupon code  I voted 17 for a 40% discount off of  your membership
 

